q('Which patient can meet the ILAE clinical definition of epilepsy after one unprovoked seizure?', 'A patient with a sufficiently high recurrence probability, at least about 60% over ten years, after appropriate assessment',[
 ('Every patient with one seizure during reversible hypoglycemia','An acute symptomatic metabolic seizure does not automatically establish epilepsy.'),('Every patient with an isolated faint and brief jerks','The event must first be established as an epileptic seizure.'),('Only someone with exactly three seizures on the same day','That is not the definition.')],
 'Epilepsy describes an enduring seizure predisposition', '**The usual pathways include two unprovoked seizures over 24 hours apart, one with high recurrence risk, or a defined epilepsy syndrome.**', ['Do not assign the high-risk estimate without evidence.','An acute symptomatic event has a different prognosis and treatment question.'], '**Diagnosis and the decision to start medication are related but distinct.**', ['Discuss recurrence, safety and patient context.','Use EEG, imaging and the clinical event description appropriately.'], [['Two unprovoked events >24 h apart','Usual diagnostic pathway'],['One high-risk event','Can qualify'],['Acute symptomatic event','Different framework']], [DE])
q('Under the ILAE 2025 seizure classification, what are the four broad classes?', 'Focal, generalized, unknown whether focal or generalized, and unclassified',[
 ('Simple, complex, grand mal and petit mal as the current formal classes','These are older or informal terms.'),('Motor, sensory, psychiatric and metabolic as the four origin classes','These mix manifestations and causes.'),('Provoked, treated, untreated and cured','These are not the seizure-classification categories.')],
 'Keep event classification separate from etiology and treatment state', '**The 2025 update preserves the broad focal/generalized framework while simplifying seizure types.**', ['Focal and unknown-origin events can be further described by consciousness.','Observable manifestations and detailed semiology add information.'], '**Use uncertainty honestly.**', ['Unknown origin is preferable to falsely labeling a bilateral convulsion as generalized from onset.','Unclassified can be appropriate when information is insufficient.'], [['Focal/generalized','Network classification'],['Unknown','Origin not determined'],['Unclassified','Insufficient categorization information']], [CL])
q('An event begins with a rising epigastric sensation, then behavioral arrest and oral automatisms with impaired responsiveness. Which classification is most compatible?', 'A focal seizure with impaired consciousness',[
 ('A typical absence solely because there is staring','The aura, sequence and automatisms favor a focal temporal event.'),('A generalized myoclonic seizure','The described event is not a brief generalized jerk.'),('A tonic seizure solely from loss of responsiveness','Impaired consciousness does not itself establish tonic motor activity.')],
 'The sequence of manifestations matters', '**An autonomic aura followed by altered consciousness can reflect temporal-network seizure activity.**', ['The 2025 classification uses consciousness, incorporating awareness and responsiveness.','Automatisms are not uniquely temporal and must be interpreted in context.'], '**Seek corroborating history and EEG/imaging.**', ['Witness descriptions and recordings can be valuable.','A normal interictal EEG does not exclude the diagnosis.'], [['Aura plus evolving sequence','Focal network clue'],['Consciousness impaired','Classification descriptor'],['Testing','Supports rather than replaces history']], [CL,NI], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A unilateral clonic event spreads and becomes a bilateral tonic–clonic convulsion. What is the appropriate classification?', 'Focal-to-bilateral tonic–clonic seizure',[
 ('Generalized tonic–clonic solely because both sides eventually move','The observed focal beginning is relevant.'),('Typical absence','This is not the described seizure type.'),('Unclassified despite a clear documented focal onset','The available information supports a more specific class.')],
 'Final appearance does not erase the beginning', '**A bilateral convulsion can arise from a focal network.**', ['The distinction can affect etiology assessment and medication choice.','If the onset was not seen, the origin may remain unknown.'], '**Document the earliest reliable manifestation.**', ['Head/eye version, focal clonus or a consistent aura can be useful.','Do not overinterpret an unreliable recollection.'], [['Clear focal beginning','Focal-to-bilateral'],['Bilateral from generalized network','Generalized class'],['Onset unseen','Unknown may be appropriate']], [CL])
q('Does a normal routine interictal EEG exclude epilepsy?', 'No; a normal study does not rule it out',[
 ('Yes, because every epilepsy produces continuous discharges','Interictal abnormalities can be intermittent or absent on a short recording.'),('Yes, unless the patient has a fever','Fever is not the deciding condition.'),('No, and therefore EEG never adds useful information','EEG can strongly support classification and diagnosis when interpreted properly.')],
 'A short sample has limited sensitivity', '**History remains central to seizure diagnosis.**', ['Sleep-deprived, ambulatory or video EEG can increase yield in selected cases.','Epileptiform activity must also be interpreted in context to avoid false diagnosis.'], '**Choose the next test for the unresolved question.**', ['A typical event capture can distinguish epileptic from nonepileptic episodes.','MRI may identify an epileptogenic structural cause.'], [['Normal routine EEG','Does not exclude'],['Abnormal EEG','Contextual support'],['Persistent uncertainty','Selected extended recording']], [NI])
q('A patient has recurrent events of uncertain nature despite several ASM trials. What test can most directly resolve whether a typical episode is epileptic?', 'Video EEG capturing the patient’s habitual event with expert clinical interpretation',[
 ('A single normal serum sodium between episodes','This does not classify the events.'),('A medication list alone','Drug exposure does not prove the diagnosis.'),('An isolated prolactin result used as universal proof','It has substantial timing and specificity limitations.')],
 'Capture the event rather than inferring it from treatment history', '**Electroclinical correlation can distinguish seizure types and functional/other events.**', ['Some focal seizures have limited scalp EEG expression, so interpretation is not merely “flat trace equals nonepileptic.”','Epilepsy and functional seizures can coexist.'], '**Use the diagnosis to reduce inappropriate escalation.**', ['A functional diagnosis needs a clear supportive explanation and treatment pathway.','Do not abruptly withdraw medicines without reviewing any coexisting epilepsy.'], [['Habitual event','Essential capture target'],['Video plus EEG','Integrated interpretation'],['Coexistence','Possible']], [NI], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Brief loss of consciousness follows prolonged standing with warmth, nausea and pallor, a few jerks and rapid recovery. What diagnosis is favored?', 'Convulsive syncope',[
 ('Epilepsy proven by any limb jerk','Syncope can include myoclonic movements.'),('Absence epilepsy solely because recovery is rapid','The trigger/prodrome and collapse differ.'),('Status epilepticus','The brief rapidly resolving episode does not fit.')],
 'Jerks do not uniquely identify an epileptic mechanism', '**Cerebral hypoperfusion can produce transient motor activity.**', ['Prodrome, posture, color and recovery are valuable clues.','Exertional episodes, cardiac symptoms or family history can signal a dangerous cardiac cause.'], '**Investigate the mechanism, not just the visible movement.**', ['An ECG is relevant after transient loss of consciousness.','Avoid unnecessary chronic ASM when the event is not epileptic.'], [['Vasovagal clues','Standing, prodrome, pallor'],['Jerks','Can occur in syncope'],['Cardiac red flags','Require targeted evaluation']], [NI], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has transient unilateral weakness after a witnessed focal-to-bilateral seizure. What is the correct interpretation?', 'Todd paresis is possible, but acute stroke must still be assessed when clinically plausible',[
 ('A preceding seizure excludes stroke','Stroke itself can provoke a seizure.'),('Every post-event weakness proves a chronic peripheral lesion','The time course suggests a central acute differential.'),('A seizure history makes all brain imaging unnecessary','New focal deficits can require urgent imaging.')],
 'A plausible postictal mechanism must not close a time-critical differential', '**Postictal weakness can last beyond the visible convulsion.**', ['The exact prior seizure pattern and recovery trajectory matter.','A new persistent deficit, vascular risks or atypical event increases stroke concern.'], '**Use the acute neurological pathway when warranted.**', ['Check glucose and obtain appropriate imaging.','Do not delay evaluation merely to see whether weakness eventually resolves.'], [['Todd paresis','Possible postictal explanation'],['Acute stroke','Can coexist or trigger seizure'],['Decision','Time-critical clinical assessment']], [NI], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A school-age child has frequent brief staring spells with abrupt recovery and generalized 3-Hz spike-wave during an event. Which syndrome is favored?', 'Childhood absence epilepsy',[
 ('Temporal lobe epilepsy as the best fit','Longer events, aura and postictal change often support a focal temporal alternative.'),('Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy solely from the word generalized','Myoclonic and generalized tonic–clonic history would be important.'),('Lennox–Gastaut syndrome as the typical 3-Hz normal-background phenotype','LGS has a different seizure/developmental and EEG pattern.')],
 'Electroclinical features define the syndrome', '**Typical absence has brief impaired consciousness with characteristic generalized spike-wave.**', ['Hyperventilation can provoke events in a suitable supervised assessment.','Development, age and other seizure types refine classification.'], '**Choose therapy for the whole seizure repertoire.**', ['Ethosuximide is appropriate when absence is the only seizure type.','Other generalized types can require a broad-spectrum alternative.'], [['Typical absence','Brief, abrupt, characteristic EEG'],['Focal event','Different sequence/recovery clues'],['Other generalized types','Change drug choice']], [TX,ref('ILAE EpilepsyDiagnosis: Childhood Absence Epilepsy','https://www.epilepsydiagnosis.org/syndrome/cae-overview.html')], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A teenager has morning myoclonic jerks, occasional generalized tonic–clonic seizures and generalized polyspike-wave, worsened by sleep deprivation. What is most likely?', 'Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy',[
 ('Typical isolated childhood absence epilepsy','The myoclonic/tonic–clonic repertoire and age favor JME.'),('A pure focal motor epilepsy established by morning timing alone','The generalized electroclinical pattern argues otherwise.'),('Lennox–Gastaut syndrome','The usual developmental and EEG phenotype differs.')],
 'Ask specifically about previously dismissed morning jerks', '**Patients may report dropping objects before recognizing the events as seizures.**', ['Sleep loss and alcohol can precipitate generalized seizures.','Absences can also occur in the syndrome.'], '**Use a suitable broad-spectrum strategy and reproductive-risk assessment.**', ['Some narrow-spectrum agents can worsen myoclonus/absence.','Counsel adherence and sleep regularity.'], [['JME clues','Morning jerks and generalized convulsions'],['EEG','Generalized polyspike-wave'],['Triggers','Sleep deprivation/alcohol']], [TX,ref('ILAE EpilepsyDiagnosis: Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy','https://www.epilepsydiagnosis.org/syndrome/jme-overview.html')], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which combination is characteristic of Lennox–Gastaut syndrome?', 'Multiple seizure types including tonic seizures, developmental impairment and slow generalized spike-wave/paroxysmal fast activity',[
 ('Only isolated brief absences with normal development and regular 3-Hz discharges','This is a different generalized epilepsy phenotype.'),('Only a focal aura with a normal background','This does not establish LGS.'),('A single acute hypoglycemic seizure','This is not a chronic developmental/epileptic syndrome.')],
 'LGS is a syndrome with an evolving electroclinical pattern', '**Generalized slow spike-wave below about 2.5 Hz and sleep-related fast activity are important EEG features.**', ['Tonic seizures are central.','The full phenotype can evolve over time.'], '**Do not diagnose from one EEG phrase alone.**', ['Etiological assessment and developmental history matter.','Treatment often requires a specialist combination and consideration of nonpharmacological strategies.'], [['Seizures','Tonic plus other types'],['EEG','Slow spike-wave/fast sleep activity'],['Development','Often impaired']], [ref('ILAE Lennox–Gastaut Diagnostic Criteria','https://www.epilepsydiagnosis.org/syndrome/lgs-diagcriteria.html')])
q('An infant has clusters of flexor spasms, developmental regression and a hypsarrhythmic EEG pattern. What is the appropriate treatment principle?', 'Urgent specialist treatment for infantile epileptic spasms syndrome rather than watchful delay',[
 ('Wait months because every infantile spasm is benign reflux','Delayed treatment can worsen developmental outcome.'),('Use a focal-seizure drug without recognizing the syndrome','Syndrome-specific first-line treatment is needed.'),('Normal development before onset excludes an epileptic encephalopathy','Regression can occur after initially typical development.')],
 'A brief movement can signal an urgent developmental disorder', '**Spasms, developmental change and the EEG pattern support the syndrome.**', ['Video of clusters can aid recognition.','Investigate structural, genetic and metabolic causes as appropriate.'], '**Start evidence-based hormonal and/or vigabatrin treatment through the specialist pathway.**', ['Tuberous sclerosis changes the preferred initial choice.','Confirm electroclinical response promptly.'], [['Spasms','Often cluster'],['Development/EEG','Key syndrome evidence'],['Action','Urgent treatment and cause assessment']], [NI,TX], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which treatment is particularly important as first-line therapy for infantile spasms associated with tuberous sclerosis?', 'Vigabatrin',[
 ('Carbamazepine as the universal spasms drug','This is not the syndrome-specific first-line choice.'),('Ethosuximide','This targets absence seizures rather than TSC-associated spasms.'),('No ASM until a second year of symptoms','This is an urgent syndrome.')],
 'Etiology can modify the first-line regimen', '**Vigabatrin has a prominent role in TSC-associated infantile spasms.**', ['Visual and other adverse effects require an appropriate monitoring discussion.','Lack of response needs rapid reassessment and treatment escalation.'], '**Treat the wider TSC context.**', ['Assess other neurological and systemic manifestations.','Response should be verified by events and EEG, not impression alone.'], [['TSC-associated spasms','Vigabatrin'],['Other spasms pathways','Hormonal/combination options'],['Response','Prompt electroclinical check']], [TX])
q('An infant develops prolonged fever-triggered hemiclonic/generalized seizures, later multiple seizure types and developmental slowing. A pathogenic SCN1A loss-of-function variant is found. Which syndrome is favored?', 'Dravet syndrome',[
 ('Uncomplicated simple febrile seizures as a complete explanation','The prolonged, evolving developmental epilepsy phenotype is more concerning.'),('Childhood absence epilepsy','Age and seizure repertoire are different.'),('Pure benign sleep myoclonus','This does not explain the severe seizure course.')],
 'A fever association can be the first sign of a genetic epilepsy', '**SCN1A-related Dravet often begins in infancy with prolonged seizures.**', ['Early development can initially be normal.','Not every SCN1A variant produces the same phenotype, so classification matters.'], '**Select syndrome-appropriate treatment.**', ['Many chronic sodium-channel blockers can worsen seizures in classic loss-of-function Dravet.','Use specialist options and an emergency rescue plan.'], [['Onset','Infancy, often fever-triggered'],['Course','Multiple seizures/developmental effects'],['Drug selection','Syndrome-specific']], [ref('ILAE EpilepsyDiagnosis: Dravet Syndrome','https://www.epilepsydiagnosis.org/syndrome/dravet-overview.html'),TX], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which chronic ASM strategy can aggravate typical absence or myoclonic seizures in an inappropriate generalized-epilepsy setting?', 'Carbamazepine or similar unsuitable narrow-spectrum sodium-channel treatment',[
 ('A properly selected broad-spectrum regimen','This is generally chosen to cover the seizure repertoire.'),('Ethosuximide in correctly identified isolated absence','This is a recognized targeted option.'),('Correcting sleep deprivation','This supports rather than aggravates seizure control.')],
 'An effective focal drug may be wrong for a generalized syndrome', '**Drug selection depends on seizure type and syndrome.**', ['A new apparent seizure type can reveal misclassification.','Some drugs have different effects on myoclonus and generalized convulsions.'], '**Reassess before declaring pharmacoresistance.**', ['The wrong drug is not an adequate correctly chosen trial.','Review the history, EEG and treatment sequence.'], [['Focal epilepsy','Some sodium-channel drugs useful'],['Absence/myoclonus','Some can worsen events'],['Failure','Recheck classification']], [TX])
q('What constitutes drug-resistant epilepsy under the ILAE definition?', 'Failure of two adequate tolerated and appropriately chosen/used ASM schedules to achieve sustained seizure freedom',[
 ('Failure of one very low dose taken intermittently','That is not an adequate trial.'),('Any two drugs prescribed for events later shown to be syncope','The diagnosis and appropriateness must be established.'),('A requirement to fail every marketed drug before referral','That delays evaluation unnecessarily.')],
 'The quality of the trials matters as much as the count', '**Misdiagnosis, nonadherence and unsuitable drug choice can create apparent resistance.**', ['Review dose, duration, tolerability and syndrome fit.','The schedules may be monotherapy or combination treatment.'], '**Refer promptly for a comprehensive tertiary evaluation.**', ['Surgery assessment is not a promise of resection.','Nonresective and diagnostic options can also improve care.'], [['Two adequate appropriate failures','Drug-resistance threshold'],['Pseudo-resistance','Check diagnosis/use'],['Next step','Tertiary evaluation']], [SU])
q('A patient with drug-resistant focal epilepsy has mesial temporal sclerosis. What is the appropriate next step?', 'Refer for comprehensive presurgical evaluation rather than endlessly cycling medicines alone',[
 ('Operate immediately from MRI alone without electroclinical assessment','Seizure localization, function and risk still need evaluation.'),('Never discuss surgery until ten drugs fail','Referral should occur much earlier.'),('Assume a visible lesion guarantees a risk-free cure','Outcome and cognitive risks require careful assessment.')],
 'A potentially resectable focus warrants an expert pathway', '**Concordant clinical, EEG and imaging evidence helps identify a surgical target.**', ['Neuropsychology and functional/anatomical assessment inform memory/language risks.','Some patients need invasive monitoring or another strategy.'], '**Distinguish referral from automatic operation.**', ['The evaluation can refine diagnosis and identify alternatives.','Shared decisions use expected seizure benefit and functional consequences.'], [['Potential focus','Assess concordance'],['Presurgical work-up','Localization and functional risk'],['Decision','Individualized benefit/risk']], [SU], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Does a normal MRI exclude a role for epilepsy surgery evaluation in drug-resistant focal epilepsy?', 'No; MRI-negative patients can still have a localizable epileptogenic network suitable for expert assessment',[
 ('Yes, because every surgical epilepsy must have an obvious lesion','Some candidates are MRI negative.'),('No, therefore every MRI-negative patient should undergo immediate resection','Localization and safety evidence are still required.'),('A normal MRI proves the events are functional','Structural imaging alone cannot establish that diagnosis.')],
 'Imaging visibility and epileptogenicity are different properties', '**Advanced imaging, prolonged EEG and selected invasive methods can add information.**', ['Re-review using an epilepsy MRI protocol can identify subtle lesions.','Not every MRI-negative case is ultimately resectable.'], '**Keep the tertiary referral threshold based on drug resistance, not one scan.**', ['Evaluation can improve care even without surgery.','Counsel honestly about uncertainty and further testing.'], [['Normal MRI','Does not exclude focal epilepsy'],['Localization','Multimodal assessment'],['Referral','Appropriate after drug resistance']], [SU])
